\section{Caching the config object}

\label{cli.utils.defaults.cache}

We keep a single [[Config]] object at module scope and populate it on
first use.
Doing the read lazily (rather than at import) keeps tests that patch
[[typerconf]] before calling [[default]] working as expected.

Constructing [[Config]] without a [[conf_file]] argument disables
automatic write-back, which is exactly what we want: the cache is
read-only by design.
The cache is deliberately \emph{not} invalidated when code elsewhere
calls [[typerconf.set]]: that call writes through its own [[Config]]
instance to persist a value for \emph{future} invocations, not to
re-read it within the same process.
Call sites that write and then read within a single run continue to
use [[typerconf.get]] directly.
<<cached config helpers>>=
_CACHE = None

def _cached_config():
  """Return a process-lifetime ``Config`` populated from disk once."""
  global _CACHE
  if _CACHE is None:
    cfg = typerconf.Config()
    cfg.read_config()
    _CACHE = cfg
  return _CACHE
@

For tests that want to exercise a different config within a single
process, we also expose a private reset hook.
<<cached config helpers>>=
def _reset_cache():
  """Drop the cached config so the next ``default`` call re-reads it."""
  global _CACHE
  _CACHE = None
@

The only cast that needs more than a direct constructor call is
[[bool]].
Python treats every non-empty string as true, so a config value like
[["false"]] would otherwise enable the option we are trying to disable.
We therefore parse the common textual forms explicitly and reject the
rest so [[default()]] can fall back safely.
<<bool parsing helper>>=
def _cast_bool(value):
  """Parse boolean config values from native or textual forms."""
  if isinstance(value, bool):
    return value
  if isinstance(value, int) and value in (0, 1):
    return bool(value)
  if isinstance(value, str):
    normalized = value.strip().lower()
    if normalized in {"1", "true", "yes", "on"}:
      return True
    if normalized in {"0", "false", "no", "off"}:
      return False
  raise ValueError(f"invalid boolean value: {value!r}")
@

\section{The [[default]] function}

Every caller spells its lookup the same way: a dotted key, a
fallback, and an optional type cast.
<<the default function>>=
def default(key, fallback, cast=None):
  """Read ``key`` from the cached config, falling back to ``fallback``.

  ``cast``, if given, is applied to the stored value; any
  ``TypeError`` or ``ValueError`` it raises is swallowed and
  ``fallback`` is returned.
  """
  <<let [[value]] be the stored value of [[key]], or return [[fallback]]>>
  if cast is None:
    return value
  try:
    if cast is bool:
      return _cast_bool(value)
    return cast(value)
  except (TypeError, ValueError):
    return fallback
<<let [[value]] be the stored value of [[key]], or return [[fallback]]>>=
for name in _stored_names(key):
  try:
    value = _cached_config().get(name)
    break
  except KeyError:
    continue
else:
  return fallback
@

\section{Reading settings stored under an old name}%
\label{cli.utils.defaults.renamed}

The configuration keys follow the commands they configure, so when [[todo ls]]
became [[todo list]] (\cref{cli.schedule.aliases}), [[todo.ls.count]] became
[[todo.list.count]].
A user's stored [[todo.ls.count]] must not silently stop applying because of
that, and it would, without any error, since a missing key is simply the
fallback.
So a lookup tries the current name first and then the old one, and the current
name wins when both are set: someone who sets the new key means it, and may
well still have the old one in the file from before.

Only reading knows about the old names.
Callers and [[--help]] say the current name, which is what
[[nytid config <key> -s <value>]] then writes.
We keep this in one table, rather than passing an old name at each of the
call sites, because whole sections were renamed: every key in the section
moved the same way, and a key added to a section later has no old name to
look for but costs nothing to check.

A malformed value under the current name still gives the fallback rather than
the old value.
Looking further would make the old key matter again exactly when the user
believes they have replaced it.
<<renamed config sections>>=
_RENAMED_SECTIONS = {
  "todo.list.": "todo.ls.",
  "track.list.": "track.ls.",
  "schedule.view.": "schedule.show.",
}

def _stored_names(key):
  """Return the names ``key`` may be stored under, current name first."""
  for current, old in _RENAMED_SECTIONS.items():
    if key.startswith(current):
      return [key, old + key[len(current):]]
  return [key]
@

Let's verify the three cases with a real [[Config]] rather than a stub, because
what matters is how [[typerconf]] answers for a section that does not exist.
<<test functions>>=
def stored_config(settings):
  """Install a real, unwritten config holding `settings` as the cache."""
  config = defaults.typerconf.Config()
  for key, value in settings.items():
    config.set(key, value)
  defaults._CACHE = config

@pytest.mark.parametrize("current, old", [
  ("todo.list.count", "todo.ls.count"),
  ("track.list.count", "track.ls.count"),
  ("schedule.view.week", "schedule.show.week"),
])
def test_default_reads_a_renamed_key(current, old):
  """The old name still applies, and the current name wins over it."""
  stored_config({})
  assert default(current, 10, int) == 10

  stored_config({old: 3})
  assert default(current, 10, int) == 3

  stored_config({current: 7})
  assert default(current, 10, int) == 7

  stored_config({old: 3, current: 7})
  assert default(current, 10, int) == 7

def test_default_does_not_read_past_a_malformed_current_key():
  """A bad value under the current name gives the fallback."""
  stored_config({"todo.ls.count": 3, "todo.list.count": "many"})
  assert default("todo.list.count", 10, int) == 10

def test_default_leaves_other_keys_alone():
  """A key outside the renamed sections has no second name."""
  stored_config({"todo.ls.count": 3, "todo.ready.count": 4})
  assert default("todo.ready.count", 10, int) == 4
  assert default("todo.view.subitems", 10, int) == 10
@

